Dear Gloria,

I hope you are doing as well as possible during this difficult time. I was very sorry to learn of your mother’s passing, and I want to extend my condolences to you and your family.

I’m writing to give you an update since our last conversation.

I believe I am currently hypomanic rather than manic. Since we last spoke and my medications were adjusted, my sleep has improved significantly. Before the medication changes, I was sleeping approximately four hours per night. I am now sleeping about seven to eight hours per night.

I do not wake up naturally after seven or eight hours. Instead, I wake up because my alarm goes off, and I am still very sleepy when I first wake up. By about 8:00 a.m., however, I generally feel fully awake. I believe I am still at risk of becoming manic, but I also believe the medication changes have helped.

One example of the impaired judgment that concerned me occurred on July 27, when I attempted to walk approximately 16 miles in 106-degree weather to have my laptop repaired. I was treated in the emergency department for dehydration. At age 62, attempting a walk of that distance in that level of heat represented extraordinarily poor judgment. I believe this was an example of impaired judgment associated with my hypomanic state.

To provide a complete picture, I have attached three documents that I prepared for Adult Protective Services:

• My letter to Adult Protective Services.
• My Chronology of Events.
• My Observations and Interpretations document, which is separate from the factual chronology.

I am not asking you to review these urgently while you are with your family. I simply wanted to keep you informed and provide the background so that, when you have time, you’ll have an accurate record of what has been happening and how I believe it has affected my mental health.

Thank you for your continued care and support. I appreciate everything you have done for me.

Sincerely,

Alex

